      <!-- Section 3: Breakthroughs -->
      <section class="content-card">
        <h2>Regional Breakthroughs &amp; Historic Firsts</h2>
        
        <h3>🌍 Africa's Historic Two-Spot Era</h3>
        <p>
          Following landmark performances by African competitors at Alexandra Palace, the PDC and the <strong>African Darts Group (ADG)</strong> have expanded the continent's representation to <strong>two guaranteed berths</strong> for the first time. Kenya's <strong>David Munyua</strong> secured the Northern Africa spot in Nairobi on 20 September 2026, edging Evans Njuguna 8–7 in a breathless deciding-leg thriller to earn back-to-back Alexandra Palace appearances. The Southern Africa qualifier is scheduled for 1 November 2026 (venue TBC).
        </p>

        <h3>🇲🇴 Macau's Historic Ally Pally Debut</h3>
        <p>
          The 2026/27 World Championship marks the tournament debut of Macau. <strong>Kam Weng Cheng</strong> made history by storming into the final of the PDC Asian Championship in Hainan, where he finished as runner-up (3–0 to champion Paolo Nebrida), becoming the first darts player from Macau ever to compete at Alexandra Palace.
        </p>

        <h3>🌏 Asian Championship &amp; Tour Dominance</h3>
        <p>
          The Asian continent sends seven confirmed competitors to London. <strong>Paolo Nebrida</strong> was crowned the 2026 PDC Asian Champion on 14 September in Hainan, defeating Kam Weng Cheng 3–0 in the final. (Defending champion Lourence Ilagan lost in the Last 16, 2–1 to Sonny Milo Balagat, and did not qualify through the 2026 Asian Championship). They are joined by the five leading Asian Tour performers: Ryuta Arihara, Christian Perez, Haruki Muramatsu, Lok Yin Lee, and Man Lok Leung (who qualified via pass-down to 7th position, as 2nd-placed Motomu Sakai qualified via Japan and 6th-placed Nebrida qualified via the Asian Championship final), alongside Indian qualifier Nitin Kumar.
        </p>

